{
 "thread": "kPthecFQ5B4",
 "subject": "Mises-Fisher distribution - multidimensional sphere",
 "messages_listed": 4,
 "last_activity": "2016-06-28T09:23:23Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "0lIozFFVCAAJ",
   "from": "Stefano",
   "author": "7a97c986864bf6d6",
   "date": "2016-06-28T03:35:16Z",
   "subject": "Mises-Fisher distribution - multidimensional sphere",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br>Hello,<div><br></div><div>I am aware that Stan includes a vonMises sampling statement <i>y ~ von_mises(mu, kappa)</i>. Is this adaptable to a Multi-dimensional sphere? i.e. Mises-Fisher generalization?</div><div><br></div><div>My goal is to create a Mises-Fisher mix model.<br></div><div><br></div><div>Thanks a lot</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "wQ3ACf5hCAAJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2016-06-28T07:27:30Z",
   "subject": "Re: Mises-Fisher distribution - multidimensional sphere",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Monday, June 27, 2016 at 11:35:16 PM UTC-4, Stefano wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I am aware that Stan includes a vonMises sampling statement <i>y ~ von_mises(mu, kappa)</i>. Is this adaptable to a Multi-dimensional sphere? i.e. Mises-Fisher generalization?</div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>No, but with Stan version 2.10.x it is easy to implement the log-PDF yourself in the Stan language, provided that either kappa is fixed or the sphere has an even number of dimensions. You just need to declare mu as a unit_vector[p] in the parameters block. If p is odd, you have to evaluate the Bessel function for a half-integer yourself. <br><br>Ben<br><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "79ZCP2hkCAAJ",
   "from": "stefano",
   "author": "7a97c986864bf6d6",
   "date": "2016-06-28T08:11:45Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Re: Mises-Fisher distribution - multidimensional sphere",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thanks Ben!<div><br></div><div>before I start I have one question: I read that the Gibbs sampling from an high dimensional mises-fisher dist is unstable </div><div><br></div><font color=\"#666666\"><i>&quot;Works in vMF have typically fo-<br>cused on low-dimensional problems (2D or 3D spaces) to<br>maintain tractability and relying on Gibbs sampling for in-<br>ference which is generally difficult to scale in higher di-<br>mensions (Hasnat et al., 2013) (Mardia &amp; El-Atoum, 1976)<br>(Guttorp &amp; Lockhart, 1988) (Bangert et al., 2010).&quot; [</i><a href=\"http://www.jmlr.org/papers/volume6/banerjee05a/banerjee05a.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.jmlr.org/papers/volume6/banerjee05a/banerjee05a.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792461036653000&amp;usg=AOvVaw03-zgpmY0dSoGXSuBbfeps\">http://www.jmlr.org/papers/volume6/banerjee05a/banerjee05a.pdf</a>]</font><div><i><br></i></div><div>Can you do a prediction for NUTS sampling?</div><div><br></div><div>If there are the basis, for creating the distribution myself I imagine I have to plug (which is not gonna be really straightforward :) ), is this the right route?</div><div><br></div><div>=======================================================</div><div><dl style=\"margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.5em;color:rgb(37,37,37);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:22.4px\"><dd style=\"margin-left:1.6em;margin-bottom:0.1em;margin-right:0px\"><img src=\"https://ci6.googleusercontent.com/proxy/DyOZzIaDQFVthyp5Lmu82l4GdZAX4F3QcfzpKjVRs9rT-fyCQTntJvx2_NOoV48vcSFIwEaJAuHn4A2QUHx8pVRTW-8w21uroYzMPbba3mVh-3bBiq37w37WyM75DgpRFBMflNENbASAxk-NpVZYnbIwQA=s0-d-e1-ft#https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/b60fc67076e711242f5a8e097cff9c12bae68499\" alt=\"{\\displaystyle f_{p}(\\mathbf {x} ;{\\boldsymbol {\\mu }},\\kappa )=C_{p}(\\kappa )\\exp \\left({\\kappa {\\boldsymbol {\\mu }}^{T}\\mathbf {x} }\\right)}\" style=\"border:none;vertical-align:-1.171ex;display:inline-block;width:31.535ex;min-height:3.509ex\"></dd></dl><p style=\"margin:0.5em 0px;line-height:22.4px;color:rgb(37,37,37);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px\">where <span style=\"overflow:hidden;width:1px;min-height:1px;font-size:16.52px\">{\\displaystyle \\kappa \\geq 0,\\left\\Vert {\\boldsymbol {\\mu }}\\right\\Vert =1\\,}</span><img src=\"https://ci6.googleusercontent.com/proxy/ytBCCrNfSRuPj9Wod_oCu1WyImJ-8LoB437H5cFtCI-yIF5MN_KY3_LzoSP1GAqGLw2HEOZ47FOG8dEWLyADbSOxBKhKIu8uS07nKQhf_ApkTaHGF8imyM7ApHrgRYTl1a0-VbW0X2__CTDrblejW3Pn2g=s0-d-e1-ft#https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/b45e4ade575c15331f71074e0b5dc082a9f99503\" alt=\"{\\displaystyle \\kappa \\geq 0,\\left\\Vert {\\boldsymbol {\\mu }}\\right\\Vert =1\\,}\" style=\"border:none;vertical-align:-0.838ex;margin:0px;display:inline-block;width:15.346ex;min-height:2.843ex\"> and the normalization constant <span style=\"overflow:hidden;width:1px;min-height:1px;font-size:16.52px\">{\\displaystyle C_{p}(\\kappa )\\,}</span><img src=\"https://ci6.googleusercontent.com/proxy/-eexwoGAZdr2A6UcNY8PPiNKzC9psoumITap43BSvQ1kQU3CSmyWJ4yFz8VjKzPnUECREGHo-6dJWjYos2KP8wagGmkt6uQM40A7ukTIgZs2nlGu-mNZGH663yklRRg71lzD26XP10OWG9A74CAY1JtzLw=s0-d-e1-ft#https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/fee113e9f0a0fa8dce9c0670d3021d9f5164f708\" alt=\"C_{p}(\\kappa )\\,\" style=\"border:none;vertical-align:-1.005ex;margin:0px;display:inline-block;width:6.306ex;min-height:3.009ex\"> is equal to</p><dl style=\"margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.5em;color:rgb(37,37,37);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:22.4px\"><dd style=\"margin-left:1.6em;margin-bottom:0.1em;margin-right:0px\"><span style=\"overflow:hidden;width:1px;min-height:1px;font-size:16.52px\">{\\displaystyle C_{p}(\\kappa )={\\frac {\\kappa ^{p/2-1}}{(2\\pi )^{p/2}I_{p/2-1}(\\kappa )}}.\\,}</span><img src=\"https://ci5.googleusercontent.com/proxy/8Jqph7t4YW8Gdw_Zu6Dp-LhuEznu47H82XXgGKYrBggnqDqlrQ1UwYhER2hR5Fh58w7FuNnxyYE5kfVPJHNxARw0B9J4SlIxgtHK-o6gnq2-JyFztexyVVgk5rfp_h6O-7-dq29cfHrYFDcsDLsLTye9qA=s0-d-e1-ft#https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/74dcc994b7867477fa0d47cb07d0634d86e40a19\" alt=\"C_{p}(\\kappa )={\\frac {\\kappa ^{p/2-1}}{(2\\pi )^{p/2}I_{p/2-1}(\\kappa )}}.\\,\" style=\"border:none;vertical-align:-3.171ex;display:inline-block;width:27.033ex;min-height:7.009ex\"></dd></dl><p style=\"margin:0.5em 0px;line-height:22.4px;color:rgb(37,37,37);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px\">where <span style=\"overflow:hidden;width:1px;min-height:1px;font-size:16.52px\">{\\displaystyle I_{v}}</span><img src=\"https://ci4.googleusercontent.com/proxy/dLssRiFVbVrddGWjBWk_5MvUrKenpX5utCSBObECeL6TeC_h9RdrxRNeYBMqSVJmHwNky7443O7af1HH6DS5KCDCJ99psLkJJgJkristhjyrT1WtGsG1gGOOwh145lM6uNlFwTJdwSkNsoGGqtiPQRusig=s0-d-e1-ft#https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/ff9de87e1fd9edc5ec418b6222c891e2f50ab62a\" alt=\"I_{v}\" style=\"border:none;vertical-align:-0.671ex;margin:0px;display:inline-block;width:2.071ex;min-height:2.509ex\"> denotes the modified <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bessel_function\" title=\"Bessel function\" style=\"text-decoration:none;color:rgb(11,0,128);background:none\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bessel_function&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792461036653000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0Esr8VoL2ajfVyOKAqZNz6\">Bessel function</a> of the first kind at order <span style=\"overflow:hidden;width:1px;min-height:1px;font-size:16.52px\">{\\displaystyle v}</span><img src=\"https://ci6.googleusercontent.com/proxy/8WQBKKD2EZnYZxMJAzvK8WGwIvBalqD_AQqKU8QWVW8GVH8_2GcoWtmy3yJDw7XaMNBwAKxQ2BKZaMjlIS0Kx24cel2I5UwiNs_uwrsxXaq3nqSTNsqVHDIlW0pGAtZzIovFGWIpJNXOnKK9MxPvLhxErA=s0-d-e1-ft#https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/e07b00e7fc0847fbd16391c778d65bc25c452597\" alt=\"v\" style=\"border:none;vertical-align:-0.338ex;margin:0px;display:inline-block;width:1.138ex;min-height:1.676ex\">. If <span style=\"overflow:hidden;width:1px;min-height:1px;font-size:16.52px\">{\\displaystyle p=3}</span><img src=\"https://ci3.googleusercontent.com/proxy/V4FrX1uvSmutfU42lTpce7P12m_B8WFvsD14qqgvgW-Q5XDi97B8DsbwLwGlSrYjOYr8iZ-4hrJzwwWcHdZycUayI_BjubxoAdOe5c4TJtPkRK8HLKzVIZeFTA868FHGm33B9HzvRjfsOKvjahPr5sxNTQ=s0-d-e1-ft#https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/b37be793795be56f61768c16dd72a1db0569fb14\" alt=\"p=3\" style=\"border:none;vertical-align:-0.671ex;display:inline-block;width:5.541ex;min-height:2.509ex\">, the normalization constant reduces to</p><p style=\"margin:0.5em 0px;line-height:22.4px;color:rgb(37,37,37);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px\"><br></p><dl style=\"margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.5em;color:rgb(37,37,37);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:22.4px\"><dd style=\"margin-left:1.6em;margin-bottom:0.1em;margin-right:0px\"><span style=\"overflow:hidden;width:1px;min-height:1px;font-size:16.52px\">{\\displaystyle C_{3}(\\kappa )={\\frac {\\kappa }{4\\pi \\sinh \\kappa }}={\\frac {\\kappa }{2\\pi (e^{\\kappa }-e^{-\\kappa })}}.\\,}</span><img src=\"https://ci5.googleusercontent.com/proxy/la05QxrDn6-DynmoNPJqPm6z_gm9Bb-JxLXj14qGJHkFQv5eoEdc9eeYGqfqf-a9jAJs3u9dnZVF3elzNORTs8aCelxSh33jHETpEI7ajN-5d6GhVAXpVmcT0Qm1te9S-IDy3u9ejGpjOr8ioUVbfoVR4A=s0-d-e1-ft#https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/75136cad19bb09910d9f18de259a7cd1e6af70e6\" alt=\"C_{3}(\\kappa )={\\frac {\\kappa }{4\\pi \\sinh \\kappa }}={\\frac {\\kappa }{2\\pi (e^{\\kappa }-e^{-\\kappa })}}.\\,\" style=\"border:none;vertical-align:-2.671ex;display:inline-block;width:36.72ex;min-height:5.509ex\"></dd><dd style=\"margin-left:1.6em;margin-bottom:0.1em;margin-right:0px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;line-height:normal\"><br></span></dd><dd style=\"margin-left:1.6em;margin-bottom:0.1em;margin-right:0px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;line-height:normal\">=======================================================</span><br></dd></dl></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><dl><dd style=\"color:rgb(37,37,37);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:22.4px;margin-left:1.6em;margin-bottom:0.1em;margin-right:0px\"><div style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;line-height:normal\"><dl style=\"margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.5em;color:rgb(37,37,37);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:22.4px\"></dl></div></dd></dl><div class=\"gmail_quote\">Thanks!</div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">2016-06-28 17:27 GMT+10:00 Ben Goodrich <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">goodri...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span>:<br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><span>On Monday, June 27, 2016 at 11:35:16 PM UTC-4, Stefano wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I am aware that Stan includes a vonMises sampling statement <i>y ~ von_mises(mu, kappa)</i>. Is this adaptable to a Multi-dimensional sphere? i.e. Mises-Fisher generalization?</div></blockquote></span><div><br>No, but with Stan version 2.10.x it is easy to implement the log-PDF yourself in the Stan language, provided that either kappa is fixed or the sphere has an even number of dimensions. You just need to declare mu as a unit_vector[p] in the parameters block. If p is odd, you have to evaluate the Bessel function for a half-integer yourself. <br><span><font color=\"#888888\"><br>Ben<br><br></font></span></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div><div>\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to a topic in the Google Groups &quot;Stan users mailing list&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this topic, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/topic/stan-users/kPthecFQ5B4/unsubscribe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/topic/stan-users/kPthecFQ5B4/unsubscribe&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792461036653000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2LEuL8fMcDHccgn558Pizs\">https://groups.google.com/d/topic/stan-users/kPthecFQ5B4/unsubscribe</a>.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and all its topics, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">stan-users+...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo post to this group, send email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">stan-...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792461036653000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0CDpD4cY7lGjJlpUQQTbOl\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.<br>\r\n</div></div></blockquote></div><br></div></div>\r\n"
